    from: MAYA,
    story: "<p>I was paged 30 times last night: the Watchdog, a warning repeated every five minutes, all straight to PagerDuty. Meanwhile, when the shop really broke last month, no alert fired for 40 minutes because the only rule looked at CPU.</p><p>Let's alert on what users feel. The shop's <b>SLO</b>: <b>99.5% of requests</b> through <code>shop.staging.kestrel.dev</code> succeed (non-5xx) over 30 days. Build the SLI recording rules and burn-rate alerts, fix Alertmanager's routing (<code>~/k8s/alerting/alertmanager.yaml</code> is the current mess), then prove it with a <b>game day</b>: break the shop on purpose, get paged, recover.</p>",
    learn:
      "<h3>SLI, SLO, error budget</h3><p>An <b>SLI</b> is a measured ratio of good events (here: non-5xx responses at the Ingress). The <b>SLO</b> is its target: 99.5% over 30 days. The <b>error budget</b> is what's left: 0.5% of requests may fail, about 3.6 hours of total outage a month. Alert when the budget is being spent too fast, not on every blip.</p>" +
      "<h3>Burn rate</h3><p>Burn rate = error ratio / budget (0.005). A burn rate of 14.4 sustained for 1 hour spends 2% of a month's budget: page someone. Use two windows so the alert fires fast <i>and</i> stops soon after recovery: the long window proves it's significant, the short one proves it's still happening.</p>" + code("- record: slo:shop_errors:ratio_rate5m\n  expr: sum(rate(nginx_ingress_controller_requests{host=\"shop...\", status=~\"5..\"}[5m]))\n      / sum(rate(nginx_ingress_controller_requests{host=\"shop...\"}[5m]))\n# ...the same for 30m, 1h, 6h\n- alert: ShopErrorBudgetBurn          # page\n  expr: slo:shop_errors:ratio_rate1h > (14.4 * 0.005) and slo:shop_errors:ratio_rate5m > (14.4 * 0.005)\n  for: 2m\n  labels: {severity: critical}\n- alert: ShopErrorBudgetBurnSlow      # ticket / Slack\n  expr: slo:shop_errors:ratio_rate6h > (6 * 0.005) and slo:shop_errors:ratio_rate30m > (6 * 0.005)\n  for: 15m\n  labels: {severity: warning}") +
      "<h3>Alertmanager</h3><p>Prometheus decides <i>what</i> is wrong; Alertmanager decides <i>who hears about it, and how often</i>. A tree of <b>routes</b> matches alert labels to receivers (first match wins unless <code>continue: true</code>); <b>group_by</b> bundles alerts into one notification; <b>inhibit_rules</b> mute warnings while a related critical alert fires; <b>repeat_interval</b> controls re-notification. Its configuration lives in the Secret <code>monitoring/alertmanager-main</code>.</p>" + ex("amtool check-config alertmanager.yaml") + ex("amtool config routes test --config.file=alertmanager.yaml severity=critical namespace=shop") + ex(AM_APPLY) + ex("oncall pages") + ex("oncall slack #shop-alerts"),
