    debrief: "<p>Now alerts mean something. The SLI measures what users get; the burn-rate alert pages only when the budget is going fast (a total outage pages within minutes, a 0.1% blip never does), and the short window makes it resolve soon after recovery. Alertmanager does the people side: the Watchdog goes to <code>null</code> (it proves the pipeline works; a dead-man's-switch service should watch it), warnings go to the team's Slack, critical pages go to the on-call, and inhibition stops the warning flood while a page is open.</p>",
    realworld: "This is the approach from Google's SRE workbook (chapter \"Alerting on SLOs\"). Sloth and Pyrra generate these recording rules and alerts from a short SLO spec; Nobl9, Grafana SLO and Datadog SLOs do it as a service. Run game days (or chaos experiments with LitmusChaos or Chaos Mesh) to prove alerts and runbooks before a real outage does.",
